<%# ── Last Release fx router ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The animation layer for the #last-release card on /deployments.

    WHY A ROUTER. The rest of LiveBoardFx is a DOM-DIFF system: the server
    broadcasts a blind "replace this slot" carrying no reason, and the client
    reverse-engineers what happened by comparing the DOM before and after. That
    inference has one default, and the default decides everything. #last-release
    defaulted to CELEBRATING — `if (!freshDeployGlow(fresh)) burst(fresh, true)` —
    so every re-render it could not explain threw confetti. DeploymentsBroadcaster
    .ci_progress re-broadcasts the release modules on every CI upsert (queued AND
    in_progress AND completed, ~24 per run per repo), and Release.last_shipped
    cannot change on a CI tick, so the card was replaced with byte-identical HTML
    and celebrated it. Every finished assembling test, a burst.

    THE INVERSION. Here nothing plays unless a handler CLAIMS the card. No claim
    is not a fallback — it is the default, and it makes a byte-identical
    re-render silent by construction rather than by guard. The bug class dies
    with the default, not with any one patch.

    THE SHAPE. Chain of responsibility, not a kind→handler map, because the
    events are not one kind of thing: `deploy.landed` is a time WINDOW that must
    resume mid-flight across a reload, while `release.swapped` is a content
    CHANGE only visible by diffing. Each handler reads the same context and
    decides for itself; the first to claim plays, alone. That "alone" is the
    anti-stampede rule — a ship and a seal landing in one beat animate once.

    THE INPUTS. Three, in falling authority:
      1. `data-fx` on the <turbo-stream> element — the server DECLARING why it
         broadcast (DeploymentsBroadcaster.release_modules(fx:)). A kind in
         SILENT_KINDS short-circuits to silence; anything else is a hint handlers
         may consult. Precedent: `data-exit-action` on the remove streams.
      2. `data-*` on the card — self-describing state. `data-fresh-deploy` +
         `data-shipped-at-ms` make the deploy glow resumable: it is a window
         measured from shipped_at by wall clock, so a reload mid-window rejoins
         at the right PHASE instead of restarting or missing it.
      3. The pre-swap snapshot — `data-card-signature`, read BEFORE Turbo
         destroys the old node, because after the swap the old values are gone.

    SCOPE. #last-release only. #current-release keeps its own three-branch
    signature guard in _deployments_live_fx (commits b0792115, 3342ee11), and the
    task cards keep the dropzone observer. Rendered by _deployments_live_fx,
    which owns the shared `.lbfx-glow` CSS this file's swap handler uses.

    The fresh-deploy window (8s in production; FRESH_DEPLOY_WINDOW_MS widens it
    for the e2e server — ApplicationHelper#fresh_deploy_window_ms). The CSS
    durations and the JS timer MUST move together: a split window would fade the
    glow at one time and flip the state at another. %>
<% fresh_window_ms = fresh_deploy_window_ms %>
<% fresh_window_css = "#{(fresh_window_ms / 1000.0).round(3)}s" %>
<style>
  /* Last Release deploy freshness: steady rainbow glow for the window's first
     half, then a fade through the second (the keyframes' 50% knee). JS sets a
     negative animation delay from data-shipped-at-ms so replacement streams
     keep the same phase instead of restarting the glow. */
  .lbfx-fresh-deploy {
    animation: lbfxFreshDeployGlow <%= fresh_window_css %> linear both;
    animation-delay: var(--lbfx-fresh-delay, 0ms);
  }
  .release-fresh-glow.lbfx-fresh-deploy::before {
    animation-name: studioBorderGlowSteam, lbfxFreshDeployRingFade;
    animation-duration: var(--studio-border-glow-duration, 20s), <%= fresh_window_css %>;
    animation-timing-function: linear, linear;
    animation-iteration-count: infinite, 1;
    animation-fill-mode: none, both;
    animation-delay: 0s, var(--lbfx-fresh-delay, 0ms);
  }
  .release-fresh-glow.lbfx-fresh-deploy::after {
    animation-name: studioBorderGlowSteam, lbfxFreshDeployHaloFade;
    animation-duration: var(--studio-border-glow-duration, 20s), <%= fresh_window_css %>;
    animation-timing-function: linear, linear;
    animation-iteration-count: infinite, 1;
    animation-fill-mode: none, both;
    animation-delay: 0s, var(--lbfx-fresh-delay, 0ms);
  }
  @keyframes lbfxFreshDeployGlow {
    0%, 50% {
      box-shadow: var(
        --task-card-glow-shadow,
        0 0 0 1px rgba(255,255,255,.72) inset,
        0 0 18px 4px rgba(248,113,113,.9),
        0 0 32px 10px rgba(250,204,21,.68),
        0 0 48px 16px rgba(52,211,153,.58),
        0 0 64px 22px rgba(96,165,250,.52),
        0 0 82px 28px rgba(192,132,252,.48)
      );
      border-color: var(--task-card-glow-border-color, rgba(255,255,255,.82));
    }
    100% {
      box-shadow:
        0 0 0 0 rgba(248,113,113,0),
        0 0 0 0 rgba(250,204,21,0),
        0 0 0 0 rgba(52,211,153,0),
        0 0 0 0 rgba(96,165,250,0),
        0 0 0 0 rgba(192,132,252,0);
      border-color: rgb(var(--lbfx-glow, 249 115 22) / 0);
    }
  }
  @keyframes lbfxFreshDeployRingFade {
    0%, 50% { opacity: var(--studio-border-glow-opacity, .9); }
    100% { opacity: 0; }
  }
  @keyframes lbfxFreshDeployHaloFade {
    0%, 50% { opacity: var(--studio-border-glow-halo-opacity, .34); }
    100% { opacity: 0; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .release-fresh-glow.lbfx-fresh-deploy::before {
      animation: lbfxFreshDeployRingFade <%= fresh_window_css %> linear both;
      animation-delay: var(--lbfx-fresh-delay, 0ms);
    }
    .release-fresh-glow.lbfx-fresh-deploy::after {
      animation: lbfxFreshDeployHaloFade <%= fresh_window_css %> linear both;
      animation-delay: var(--lbfx-fresh-delay, 0ms);
    }
  }
</style>
<script>
  // Installed once; survives Turbo navigations.
  window.ReleaseFx = window.ReleaseFx || (function () {
    const CARD_ID = "last-release";
    const FRESH_DEPLOY_MS = <%= fresh_window_ms %>; // fresh_deploy_window_ms — same value as the CSS above
    // Everything the fresh glow writes inline, so clearing it leaves no residue.
    const FRESH_DEPLOY_STYLE_PROPS = [
      "--lbfx-fresh-delay",
      "--studio-border-glow-offset",
      "--studio-border-glow-duration",
      "--studio-border-glow-angle",
      "--task-card-glow-color",
      "--task-card-glow-color-a",
      "--task-card-glow-color-b",
      "--task-card-glow-border-color",
      "--task-card-glow-shadow",
      "border-color",
      "box-shadow"
    ];
    const SWAP_GLOW_MS = 850; // matches the .lbfx-glow animation in _deployments_live_fx

    // Declared kinds that mean "I re-broadcast, but nothing about THIS card moved."
    // A short-circuit, not a handler: it stops even the synthesis path from
    // inferring an event out of a redraw the server has already told us is inert.
    // `ci.progress` is the one that caused the original bug — kept declared (and
    // kept honoured) even though .ci_progress no longer pushes this slot at all,
    // so the silence survives a future caller wiring it back up.
    const SILENT_KINDS = new Set(["ci.progress"]);

    // ── handlers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Each: claims(ctx) → may I play? · play(card) → true if I did.
    // Order is priority. The FIRST to claim plays, and nothing else does.

    // A deploy just landed in this slot. A WINDOW, not an instant: it is claimed
    // for FRESH_DEPLOY_MS after shipped_at, and re-entered (not restarted) on every
    // render inside that window — a negative animation-delay rejoins the CSS at the
    // phase the wall clock says it should be at.
    function freshDeployGlow(card) {
      if (!card || card.dataset.freshDeploy !== "true") return false;
      const shippedAt = Number(card.dataset.shippedAtMs || 0);
      const elapsed = shippedAt > 0 ? Math.max(0, Date.now() - shippedAt) : 0;
      if (elapsed >= FRESH_DEPLOY_MS) {
        clearFreshDeployGlow(card);
        return false;
      }
      if (card.dataset.freshDeployCleanup === "true") return true;

      card.style.setProperty("--lbfx-fresh-delay", "-" + elapsed + "ms");
      card.classList.remove("opacity-75");
      card.classList.add("studio-border-glow", "release-fresh-glow");
      card.classList.remove("lbfx-fresh-deploy");
      void card.offsetWidth;
      card.classList.add("lbfx-fresh-deploy");
      card.dataset.freshDeployCleanup = "true";
      setTimeout(() => clearFreshDeployGlow(card), FRESH_DEPLOY_MS - elapsed + 150);
      return true;
    }

    function clearFreshDeployGlow(card) {
      if (!card) return;
      card.classList.remove("lbfx-fresh-deploy", "studio-border-glow", "release-fresh-glow");
      card.classList.add("opacity-75");
      card.dataset.freshDeploy = "false";
      delete card.dataset.freshDeployCleanup;
      FRESH_DEPLOY_STYLE_PROPS.forEach((prop) => card.style.removeProperty(prop));
      if (!card.getAttribute("style")) card.removeAttribute("style");
    }

    // A DIFFERENT release now occupies the slot (a ship outside the fresh window,
    // an archive sweep repointing last_shipped, a member list that moved). One
    // subtle glow — the honest version of what the old burst was aiming at and
    // never once correctly hit, since a real deploy always claims the handler
    // above and only a NON-event could reach the fallback.
    function swapGlow(card) {
      const rgb = hexToRgb(card.dataset.glow || "");
      if (rgb) card.style.setProperty("--lbfx-glow", rgb);
      card.classList.add("lbfx-glow");
      setTimeout(() => {
        card.classList.remove("lbfx-glow");
        card.style.removeProperty("--lbfx-glow");
      }, SWAP_GLOW_MS);
      return true;
    }

    // "#78C850" → "120 200 80" (for `rgb(var() / a)`); null if unparseable.
    function hexToRgb(hex) {
      const m = /^#?([0-9a-f]{6})$/i.exec(hex || "");
      if (!m) return null;
      const n = parseInt(m[1], 16);
      return ((n >> 16) & 255) + " " + ((n >> 8) & 255) + " " + (n & 255);
    }

    // ── the registry ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Adding an animation means adding a row here. A row that claims nothing
    // plays nothing, and a card no row claims stays still.
    const HANDLERS = [
      { kind: "deploy.landed",   resumable: true,  claims: (ctx) => ctx.card.dataset.freshDeploy === "true", play: freshDeployGlow },
      { kind: "release.swapped", resumable: false, claims: (ctx) => !!ctx.before && ctx.signature !== ctx.before.signature, play: swapGlow }
    ];

    // ── routing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    // Everything worth remembering about the slot BEFORE Turbo replaces it. Taken
    // in ONE call so every reading comes from the same render.
    function snapshot(card) {
      if (!card) return null;
      return { signature: card.dataset.cardSignature || "" };
    }

    // Route one render of the card. `before` is null on a plain page load (nothing
    // was replaced), which is exactly right: the resumable handler still claims a
    // live window, and the diff-based one cannot claim without a diff.
    // Returns the kind that played, or null for silence.
    function route(card, before, declared) {
      if (!card) return null;
      if (SILENT_KINDS.has(declared)) return null;
      const ctx = { card: card, before: before, declared: declared, signature: card.dataset.cardSignature || "" };
      for (const handler of HANDLERS) {
        if (!handler.claims(ctx)) continue;
        return handler.play(card) ? handler.kind : null;
      }
      return null;
    }

    // Re-enter any resumable fx that is still inside its window. Runs on page load
    // and Turbo navigation, where there is no swap to route — the glow belongs to
    // the release's wall clock, not to the render that happened to show it.
    function resume(root) {
      const scope = root && root.querySelectorAll ? root : document;
      scope.querySelectorAll("#" + CARD_ID).forEach((card) => route(card, null, null));
    }

    function scheduleResume() {
      requestAnimationFrame(() => resume(document));
    }

    document.addEventListener("turbo:load", scheduleResume);
    if (document.readyState === "loading") {
      document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", scheduleResume, { once: true });
    } else {
      scheduleResume();
    }

    return { CARD_ID, snapshot, route, resume, HANDLERS, SILENT_KINDS };
  })();
</script>
